C = [
("h1", "6. Tyre and Sidon"),
("p", "Claim: Tyre is the hardest case in the series, and the text itself explains why. Isaiah gives it a term of seventy years and a trade that ends “holiness to the LORD.” Ezekiel says it shall be built no more and never found again, names Nebuchadrezzar as the first to come against it, and then, sixteen years later, reports that the siege paid him nothing and gives him Egypt instead. Zechariah, later still, finds the city rich again and says the LORD will cast her out. The volume reports these statements side by side as written, adds no timeline the text does not give, and classes Tyre as part-fulfilled with an open end. The reading does not call Ezekiel 26 a failed prediction, and it does not call it a finished one."),
("sub", "6.1 Isaiah: the crowning city and the seventy years"),
("p", "The burden opens with a call to the ships. “The burden of Tyre. Howl, ye ships of Tarshish; for it is laid waste, so that there is no house, no entering in” (Isaiah 23:1). The city’s place in the world is told in the next verses: “she is a mart of nations” (Isaiah 23:3). The question of verse 8 is who has done this: “Who hath taken this counsel against Tyre, the crowning city, whose merchants are princes, whose traffickers are the honourable of the earth?” The answer is the one the oracles of this volume keep giving: “The LORD of hosts hath purposed it, to stain the pride of all glory, and to bring into contempt all the honourable of the earth” (Isaiah 23:9). The ground is pride in glory and honour. The LORD “stretched out his hand over the sea, he shook the kingdoms: the LORD hath given a commandment against the merchant city, to destroy the strong holds thereof” (Isaiah 23:11)."),
("p", "Then comes the term. “And it shall come to pass in that day, that Tyre shall be forgotten seventy years, according to the days of one king: after the end of seventy years shall Tyre sing as an harlot” (Isaiah 23:15). The seventy years are of forgetting, not of ruin. After them: “And it shall come to pass after the end of seventy years, that the LORD will visit Tyre, and she shall turn to her hire, and shall commit fornication with all the kingdoms of the world upon the face of the earth” (Isaiah 23:17). The text calls her trade fornication. The next verse is the oracle’s last: “And her merchandise and her hire shall be holiness to the LORD: it shall not be treasured nor laid up; for her merchandise shall be for them that dwell before the LORD, to eat sufficiently, and for durable clothing” (Isaiah 23:18). Tyre’s trade becomes provision for the LORD’s own people. The sentence ends with the city restored to commerce and the commerce given a holy use."),
("sub", "6.2 Ezekiel 26: many nations, and one king"),
("p", "Ezekiel states the ground in the city’s own word, the one Ammon used: “Aha, she is broken that was the gates of the people: she is turned unto me: I shall be replenished, now she is laid waste” (Ezekiel 26:2). The LORD’s answer is, “Behold, I am against thee, O Tyrus, and will cause many nations to come up against thee, as the sea causeth his waves to come up” (Ezekiel 26:3). The figure is of waves, one after another. Verse 4 sets out the work: “And they shall destroy the walls of Tyrus, and break down her towers: I will also scrape her dust from her, and make her like the top of a rock.” And verse 5: “It shall be a place for the spreading of nets in the midst of the sea.”"),
("p", "Then the person changes. “For thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I will bring upon Tyrus Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, a king of kings, from the north” (Ezekiel 26:7). The verbs of verses 7 to 11 are in the singular, “he”: he shall slay the daughters in the field, make a fort, cast a mount, set engines against the walls. In verse 12 they turn plural again: “And they shall make a spoil of thy riches, and make a prey of thy merchandise: and they shall break down thy walls.” The chapter then says the LORD will cause the noise of the songs to cease (26:13), and, “And I will make thee like the top of a rock: thou shalt be a place to spread nets upon; thou shalt be built no more: for I the LORD have spoken it” (Ezekiel 26:14). The end of the chapter is the sharpest. The LORD will “bring thee down with them that descend into the pit” (Ezekiel 26:20) and say, “I will make thee a terror, and thou shalt be no more: though thou be sought for, yet shalt thou never be found again, saith the Lord GOD” (Ezekiel 26:21)."),
("p", "Two observations arise from the grammar of the chapter. The first is that the sentence names a first stroke, Nebuchadrezzar, and a larger sweep, “many nations,” and keeps them in different persons. The second is that the spoil of verse 12 is the work of “they,” not “he.” The volume notes both and builds on neither a claim about what Nebuchadrezzar took. Critics who read the chapter as a failed prediction take verses 7 to 14 together as one prediction of a single conquest with a single prize. Paul Ferguson, who answers them, takes the opposite course and says “The passage does not state that Nebuchadnezzar would capture the island city and get its wealth” (//Bible and Spade//, 2006). The volume’s own claim is narrower than either. It is that the text, in Ezekiel 29:17–20, gives its own report of what the siege yielded, and that this report is a thing the first chapter did not have to say."),
("sub", "6.3 The prince of Tyrus"),
("p", "Between the two statements Ezekiel speaks to the ruler. “Son of man, say unto the prince of Tyrus, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because thine heart is lifted up, and thou hast said, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas; yet thou art a man, and not God” (Ezekiel 28:2). The prince is told in irony, “Behold, thou art wiser than Daniel” (Ezekiel 28:3), and the strangers who come will “defile thy brightness” (Ezekiel 28:7). His end is a man’s: “Thou shalt die the deaths of the uncircumcised by the hand of strangers: for I have spoken it, saith the Lord GOD” (Ezekiel 28:10). The volume reads these ten verses as the LORD’s word to the ruler of Tyre. The lamentation that follows (Ezekiel 28:11–19) is spoken to the king of Tyre, and the volume does not read it as pointing to any unseen being. That question is not this volume’s, and the volume names no one beyond the king the prophet addresses."),
("p", "At the end of chapter 28 the oracle turns to the neighbours and to Israel. “And there shall be no more a pricking brier unto the house of Israel, nor any grieving thorn of all that are round about them, that despised them” (Ezekiel 28:24). “And they shall dwell safely therein, and shall build houses, and plant vineyards; yea, they shall dwell with confidence, when I have executed judgments upon all those that despise them round about them” (Ezekiel 28:26). The judgment on the neighbours has a purpose that is stated: the safety of Israel in her land. The statement is read again in chapter 8."),
("sub", "6.4 The wages of the siege"),
("p", "Ezekiel 29:17–20 is the text updating its own report. “Son of man, Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon caused his army to serve a great service against Tyrus: every head was made bald, and every shoulder was peeled: yet had he no wages, nor his army, for Tyrus, for the service that he had served against it” (Ezekiel 29:18). The prophet puts the siege in the past tense: it has been served, and the pay has not come. The substitution follows (Ezekiel 29:19, 20). The volume reads two things in this. The first is that Ezekiel does not say the siege failed. The service was “a great service,” and the men were worn by it. The second is that the LORD does not say he had promised wages for it in chapter 26. He supplies them from a different quarter, and says why: “because they wrought for me” (Ezekiel 29:20). The siege was for the LORD, and the LORD pays."),
("p", "Robert Carroll, Edwin Burtt and Keith Carley are named in Ferguson’s article among those who treat the Tyre prophecies as unfulfilled or as a problem, and Ferguson states the critical reading in these terms: “Ezekiel 29:17–20 is an apology by the prophet for being wrong” (//Bible and Spade//, 2006). The volume knows these three only through Ferguson’s report and says what they hold in its own words. Ferguson’s reply is that the passages do not conflict: “There is no contradiction.” Hank Hanegraaff, in the //Christian Research Journal// in 2013, writes of the same siege, “His efforts, however, went largely unrewarded,” and then finds the full fulfilment of the prophecy in Alexander’s capture of the island city, writing “Thus was fulfilled the most improbable of prophecies.” The volume agrees with them on the facts of Ezekiel 29:18: the service went unrewarded. It does not take them in their conclusions, that the whole of chapter 26 is done, or that the prophet has erred. Chapter 9 sets out the disagreement."),
("sub", "6.5 Zechariah: the city built again"),
("p", "Zechariah’s burden is later than Ezekiel’s and speaks of the city as it stands again. “And Tyrus did build herself a strong hold, and heaped up silver as the dust, and fine gold as the mire of the streets” (Zechariah 9:3). The sentence follows at once: “Behold, the Lord will cast her out, and he will smite her power in the sea; and she shall be devoured with fire” (Zechariah 9:4). The oracle goes on down the coast, to Ashkelon, Gaza and Ekron (Zechariah 9:5–7), and turns to the house of the LORD: “And I will encamp about mine house because of the army … and no oppressor shall pass through them any more: for now have I seen with mine eyes” (Zechariah 9:8). The oracle puts Tyre’s fall inside a word about Judah’s safety, as Ezekiel 28:24–26 did."),
("p", "Two things follow for the volume. A word of the LORD speaks against Tyre after Ezekiel’s, so the ending of Tyre was not closed by Ezekiel 26 and the text does not say it was. And Zechariah 9:4 has no later fulfilment reported in Scripture, so it stands among the open sentences. The volume cannot say that Zechariah 9:4 was Alexander’s siege, and cannot say it was not; it reports the verse and says the text gives no date for it."),
("sub", "6.6 Joel, Amos and the Lord’s own words"),
("p", "Two other prophets and the Lord himself name Tyre and Sidon. Amos charges Tyre with delivering up “the whole captivity to Edom” and forgetting “the brotherly covenant” (Amos 1:9), and sends “a fire on the wall of Tyrus” (Amos 1:10). Joel has the LORD say, “Yea, and what have ye to do with me, O Tyre, and Zidon, and all the coasts of Palestine?” (Joel 3:4). The charge is the theft of the temple’s silver and gold, “my goodly pleasant things,” and the sale of Judah’s children (Joel 3:5, 6). The sentence is a reversal: the LORD will “sell your sons and your daughters into the hand of the children of Judah, and they shall sell them to the Sabeans, to a people far off” (Joel 3:8). It is set in the day when the nations are gathered (Joel 3:2), which chapter 7 takes up."),
("p", "In the Gospels the Lord names the two cities in a comparison. To the cities that did not repent he says, “if the mighty works, which were done in you, had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes,” and, “It shall be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon at the day of judgment, than for you” (Matthew 11:21, 22). Luke gives the same saying (Luke 10:13, 14). The Gospel Commentary volume on the Lord’s ministry to Israel reads the passage for Chorazin and Bethsaida. This volume draws one point: the Lord speaks of Tyre and Sidon as places whose people stand at a coming judgment, and he does not say the cities stand or lie in ruins. The volume leaves it there."),
("ctbl", "Tyre in four questions.", [
    ("Question", "The text", "Passage"),
    ("Against whom?", "Tyre and Sidon; the prince of Tyrus", "Isaiah 23:1; Ezekiel 26:3; 28:2; Zechariah 9:2"),
    ("What ground?", "Pride in glory; “Aha” over Jerusalem; the sale of captives; the temple’s silver", "Isaiah 23:9; Ezekiel 26:2; Amos 1:9; Joel 3:5, 6"),
    ("What term and instrument?", "Seventy years’ forgetting; Nebuchadrezzar; many nations like waves", "Isaiah 23:15; Ezekiel 26:3, 7"),
    ("What the text reports", "The siege paid no wages; Egypt is given in place of Tyre’s spoil", "Ezekiel 29:17–20"),
    ("What stays open", "Her trade as “holiness to the LORD”; “devoured with fire”; “no more” and “never be found again”", "Isaiah 23:18; Zechariah 9:4; Ezekiel 26:14, 21"),
    ("Class", "Part-fulfilled, with an open end", "Isaiah 23; Ezekiel 26–29; Zechariah 9"),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "6.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Isaiah 23 gives Tyre a term of seventy years’ forgetting, a return to trade and a hire that is “holiness to the LORD” (Isaiah 23:15–18).",
    "Ezekiel 26 names a first stroke, Nebuchadrezzar, and a larger sweep, “many nations,” and ends with “built no more” and “never be found again.”",
    "Ezekiel 29:17–20 is the text’s own report that the siege yielded no wages and that Egypt is given in their place; the volume reads it as the prophet’s report and not as a retraction.",
    "Zechariah 9:3, 4 speaks of Tyre as strong again and as cast out and devoured with fire; the verse has no later fulfilment reported.",
    "The volume holds Isaiah 23:17, 18, Ezekiel 26:21 and Zechariah 9:4 side by side as written, adds no timeline, and names no unseen being behind the prince of Tyrus.",
  ]),
]
